  "strategy": "# Approach and Rationale\nThis approach combines structural candidate generation with multi-phase optimization to reduce the infidelity metric below 1.504889. Instead of relying solely on systematic length exploration, the key idea is to optimize sequences with varying numbers of peaks by probabilistically adjusting peak positions and widths through the Boltzmann distribution framework. This maintains the benefits of temperature-weighted selection while introducing more randomness in the exploration phase.\n\nThe central problem of minimizing two-bombed infidelity is addressed through six distinct optimization methods:\n1. Direct subspace optimization using projected gradient descent\n2. Barrier-LBFGS refinement with deterministic annealing\n3. Genetic evolution with crossover and mutation operators\n4. Gaussian process-based Bayesian optimization\n5. Coordinate-wise hill climbing with dynamic subset selection\n6. Reinforcement learning using sliding window average as reward\n\nThe implementation prioritizes three primary search dimensions: length exploration (512-1500 points), optimization methodology, and peak placement strategies. Computational cost is controlled by adaptive temperature, dimensionality, and evaluation density.\n\n## Phase 1: Multi-Method Optimization (40% budget)\nWe sample 8-24 candidates per length configuration (powers of two). For each candidate, we apply an optimization sequence including:\n- Initial coordinate scaling (step size = 0.3)\n- Adaptive temperature gradient search (500 iterations, cooling factor)\n- Multiple restarts at convergence\n- Quick refinement at final stages\n\nThe first phase uses step function interpolation to maintain discrete structures during optimization.\n\n## Phase 2: Progressive Architectural Divergence (30% budget)\nWe implement a greedy search where each phase uses the best candidate from previous iteration to generate new architectures. For each new sequence:\n- We ensure the sum \u2265 0.001 through clipping and restarting\n- We use normalized interpolations to maintain scale invariance\n- We reset temperature after major re-architectures to \u03b8_min\u2192\u03b8_max\n\n## Phase 3: Reinforcement and Hyperparameter Sweeps (20% budget)\nParameters are mapped to hyperparameters via ReLU/sigmoid activation at each step. Theoptimizer's dimension is dynamically adjusted based on the ratio of time remaining to the largest dimension in the current length.\n\n## Phase 4: Candidate Finalization and Fallback\nAfter each segment, we verify the candidate's infidelity with multiple evaluations before acceptance. If the best candidate emerges during multi-phase optimization, we use evaluate_sequence() to determine if any remaining time can achieve improvement.\n\nThe approach leverages:\n\u2022 Length-range: 512-5000 (prioritizing powers of two for FFT)\n\u2022 Peak configuration: 2-16 (depending on length)\n\u2022 Temperature: Initial 0.002\u2192 Final 0.4\n\u2022 Time management: Strict checks occurring at each initial placement and evaluation.",
